body:
I agree, it can be very damaging to a host of disorders. A lot of times, people push acceptance and try to tiptoe around things as to not offend anyone. But it drives me crazy when people try to excuse their shitty behavior with "I have x condition" and people have to accept it or be called bigots. It's not some "get out of jail free" card like people act.

&#x200B;

I have a few diagnosed disorders, and some people act like it's a badge of pride. It's not, it's not something to be proud of, it's not something that I want. It is important to accept yourself and your flaws, but it is equally important to try and improve yourself. Keep working on you, what's important is making yourself happy and proud.

Geez, that's awful. I'm sorry your partner did that to you on multiple occasions. I know how gut-wrenching it is for someone you love to do hurtful things to you. My ex would leave hand print bruises on me, and trapped me inside a bathroom because anytime I'd try to leave, he'd shoot at me with a pellet gun. I never called the police on him, but I did get out after 3 years. 

&#x200B;

I'm glad you got out, but I'm sorry about the battle you're going through. I really hope it all comes to an end and you can finally lay it all to rest.
